+    "title": "Describing emotions: verb or adjective plus the right preposition",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** use the common preposition that follows an emotion word; choose between *with*, *about*, *at*, *of*, *in* and *on*; write sentences that describe how a person feels and why.\n\n**Teaching:** Many words that name feelings need a particular preposition before the thing that causes the feeling. The preposition is part of the expression and must be learned with the word. The same feeling can take different prepositions for a person and for a thing.\n\n- afraid of, scared of, frightened of, proud of, ashamed of, jealous of, fond of, tired of, full of\n- angry **with** a person, angry **about** or **at** a thing\n- worried about, excited about, upset about, anxious about\n- surprised at or by, shocked at or by, amazed at or by\n- disappointed with or in a person, disappointed **at** or **about** a result\n- pleased with, satisfied with, happy with, bored with\n- interested in, keen on, good at, sorry for a person, sorry about a thing\n\nAfter a preposition use a noun, a pronoun or the **-ing** form: “She is afraid of the dark”; “He is proud of winning”. Do not use the infinitive after the preposition (“proud to winning” is wrong).\n\n**Worked example:** “Ngozi was disappointed with her result because she was worried about the mathematics paper.” Here *with* links the feeling to the thing she received and *about* links worry to the paper.\n\n**Practice:** Fill each gap with a preposition. (1) The captain was proud ___ his team. (2) Bola is angry ___ her brother for losing the book. (3) I am fond ___ roasted plantain. (4) We were surprised ___ the sudden rain. (5) The class is excited ___ the trip. (6) Tunde is not interested ___ football. (7) She felt ashamed ___ shouting at the guest. (8) Write two sentences of your own describing an emotion and its cause, using a different preposition in each.\n\n**Answers:** (1) of (2) with (3) of (4) at or by (5) about (6) in (7) of. (8) Marking guide: one mark for a correct emotion word, one for the correct following preposition, one for a noun or -ing form after it; award no mark for a sentence that changes the preposition to fit the sentence without checking the expression.\n\n**Assessment:** 7 marks for the gaps and 6 for the sentences. Written grammar does not by itself show spoken fluency; check use in speech separately.",
