+    "title": "Changing a positive statement into a negative statement",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** turn a positive sentence into a negative sentence in the present, past, future and perfect forms; use contractions correctly; change *some* words and other positive words to their negative partners.\n\n**Teaching:** Negatives are formed by adding **not** after the first auxiliary verb. If the sentence has only a main verb in the simple present or simple past, add **do not / does not / did not** and use the base form of the verb.\n\n- **be:** “She is late” becomes “She is not (isn't) late.”\n- **simple present:** “They play football” becomes “They do not (don't) play football”; “He plays” becomes “He does not (doesn't) play.”\n- **simple past:** “We finished” becomes “We did not (didn't) finish.” After *did not* the main verb returns to its base form.\n- **will, can, must, should and other modals:** “I can swim” becomes “I cannot (can't) swim”; “She will come” becomes “She will not (won't) come.”\n- **perfect and continuous forms:** put *not* after the first auxiliary: “has not eaten”, “was not reading”.\n\nSome positive words change as well: *some* becomes **any**, *something* becomes **anything**, *someone* becomes **anyone**, *always* becomes **never** and *already* becomes **yet**. Standard English does not use two negatives together: say “I did not see anyone” or “I saw no one”, not “I did not see no one”.\n\n**Worked example:** “Ada has already cooked some rice.” Negative: “Ada has not cooked any rice yet.”\n\n**Practice:** Change to the negative. (1) The headmaster is angry. (2) Uche sings in the choir. (3) They arrived early. (4) I will buy something. (5) The children can read the notice. (6) Segun has finished the homework already. (7) She always walks to school.\n\n**Answers:** (1) The headmaster is not angry. (2) Uche does not sing in the choir. (3) They did not arrive early. (4) I will not buy anything. (5) The children cannot read the notice. (6) Segun has not finished the homework yet. (7) She never walks to school. Accept correct contractions. Marking: one mark each; deduct nothing for choosing *cannot* or *can't*.\n\n**Assessment:** 7 marks. Ask learners to say each sentence aloud so the contraction is practised, because a written correction does not establish spoken accuracy.",
