+    "title": "Question tags: checking and confirming",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** add a correct question tag to a statement; use the right polarity; handle the common irregular cases; read the tune of a tag as either checking or confirming.\n\n**Teaching:** A question tag is a short question added to the end of a statement. Use the same auxiliary (or *be*, or *do*) as in the statement and the matching pronoun. A **positive** statement usually takes a **negative** tag, and a **negative** statement takes a **positive** tag.\n\n- “You are ready, **aren't you**?” / “You aren't ready, **are you**?”\n- “Tola sings well, **doesn't she**?” (simple present uses *do/does*)\n- “They left early, **didn't they**?” (simple past uses *did*)\n- “She will come, **won't she**?” / “You can swim, **can't you**?”\n- “I am late, **aren't I**?” (the standard tag for *I am*)\n- “Let's go, **shall we**?” and “Open the door, **will you**?” (suggestions and commands)\n- “Nobody called, **did they**?” — words such as *nobody*, *no one*, *never* and *hardly* make the statement negative, so the tag is positive and *they* is used for *nobody/everybody/someone*.\n\nTune: when the voice falls on the tag the speaker expects agreement; when it rises the speaker is asking a real question. In speech work listen for the fall or rise.\n\n**Worked example:** “Your brother plays the drums, ____?” The verb is *plays* (simple present, positive), so the tag is negative: “doesn't he?”\n\n**Practice:** Add a tag. (1) The lesson started at eight, ____? (2) You haven't seen my pen, ____? (3) Ife is your cousin, ____? (4) Let's sit here, ____? (5) Nobody told the teacher, ____? (6) I am your friend, ____? (7) Say a sentence aloud with the tag falling, then rising, and explain the difference.\n\n**Answers:** (1) didn't it (2) have you (3) isn't she (4) shall we (5) did they (6) aren't I (7) Falling tune: checking something the speaker believes; rising tune: a genuine question. Marking: one mark each for (1)–(6) and two for (7).\n\n**Assessment:** 8 marks. Pronunciation of the tune must be judged by listening; a written answer cannot show it.",
