+    "title": "Conjunctions: joining words, phrases and clauses",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** name the three kinds of conjunction; choose a suitable conjunction for the relationship between two ideas; punctuate the joined sentence correctly.\n\n**Teaching:** A conjunction joins words, phrases or clauses.\n\n- **Coordinating** conjunctions join equal parts: *and* (addition), *but* (contrast), *or* (choice), *so* (result), *for* (reason), *nor*, *yet*. Use a comma before them when they join two full clauses: “The bell rang, so we went in.”\n- **Subordinating** conjunctions begin a dependent clause: *because* and *since* (reason), *although* and *though* (contrast), *if* and *unless* (condition), *when*, *while*, *after*, *before* and *until* (time), *so that* (purpose). The dependent clause cannot stand alone: “Although it rained, we played.”\n- **Correlative** pairs: *either … or*, *neither … nor*, *both … and*, *not only … but also*.\n\nA subordinating clause at the start of a sentence is followed by a comma; at the end, usually no comma. Do not use *although* and *but* together in one sentence.\n\n**Worked example:** Join “The road was flooded” and “we arrived on time.” With *although*: “Although the road was flooded, we arrived on time.” With *but*: “The road was flooded, but we arrived on time.”\n\n**Practice:** Choose the best conjunction in brackets. (1) He stayed at home (because / although) he was ill. (2) You may have tea (or / but) water. (3) (Unless / If) you study, you will pass. (4) She is clever (and / but) lazy. (5) Join with *so that*: “Kunle woke early. He would not miss the bus.” (6) Write one sentence using *neither … nor*.\n\n**Answers:** (1) because (2) or (3) If (4) but (5) Kunle woke early so that he would not miss the bus. (6) Marking guide: both parts must be parallel, for example “She likes neither rice nor beans.” One mark each, two for (6).\n\n**Assessment:** 7 marks.",
