+    "title": "Prefixes, suffixes and synonyms: building and choosing words",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** build new words with prefixes and suffixes; explain how a prefix or suffix changes meaning or word class; choose a synonym that fits the context.\n\n**Teaching:** A **prefix** is added before a root and usually changes meaning: *un-* and *dis-* (opposite: *unhappy*, *disagree*), *re-* (again: *rewrite*), *mis-* (wrongly: *misspell*), *pre-* (before: *prepare*), *inter-* (between: *interschool*). A **suffix** is added after a root and often changes the word class: *-ness* (noun: *kindness*), *-ful* (adjective: *careful*), *-ly* (adverb: *slowly*), *-ment* (noun: *enjoyment*), *-able* (adjective: *readable*), *-er/-or* (a person who: *teacher*, *visitor*). A word made this way is a **derivative**. Spelling may change: *happy* becomes *happiness*.\n\n**Synonyms** are words with nearly the same meaning, but they are seldom identical. Choose by context and tone: *big*, *large* and *enormous*; *begin* and *commence* (formal). A synonym must also fit the grammar: “She was glad” can be “She was pleased”, but “glad with” is not the usual pairing.\n\n**Worked example:** *Help* + *less* = *helpless* (adjective: without help); *help* + *ful* = *helpful*. “The teacher was **pleased** with the work” can use the synonym **satisfied**, but not **happy for the work**.\n\n**Practice:** (1) Add a prefix to make the opposite: *possible, honest, agree*. (2) Add a suffix to make a noun from *kind, govern, enjoy*. (3) Give the word class of *carefully* and *carefulness*. (4) Replace the underlined word with a suitable synonym: “The principal was **angry** with the noisy students.” (5) Explain why *thin* and *slim* are not always interchangeable.\n\n**Answers:** (1) impossible, dishonest, disagree. (2) kindness, government, enjoyment. (3) adverb; noun. (4) furious, annoyed or cross. (5) *Slim* is usually complimentary; *thin* can suggest too little weight. Marking: one mark per item; accept any suitable synonym in (4).\n\n**Assessment:** 12 marks. A dictionary with example sentences is the best check for any doubtful synonym.",
