+    "title": "Consonant practice: /ʒ/ and /dʒ/; /d/, /ð/, /θ/ and /z/; /w/ and /j/",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** hear and produce /ʒ/ against /dʒ/; separate /d/, /ð/, /θ/ and /z/; separate /w/ from /j/; use minimal pairs for self-correction.\n\n**Note on the scheme:** the original document prints these items in plain ASCII (“/3/ and /d3/”, “/d/, /0/, /z/”). They are read here as /ʒ/ and /dʒ/ (a published text form of the symbols) and, by inference, as /d/, /θ/ and /z/ (the zero as θ). The author's exact intent is not recovered; the related voiced sound /ð/ is added because it is the usual neighbour of /θ/ and /d/.\n\n**Teaching:** (1) /ʒ/ is a continuous, voiced friction sound as in *vision, measure, leisure, television*; /dʒ/ begins with a tongue closure and then releases friction as in *jam, judge, age, bridge*. Minimal pair: **leisure** /ˈleʒə/ and **ledger** /ˈledʒə/. (2) /θ/ is voiceless (*thin, think, three, bath*) and /ð/ is voiced (*this, that, they, breathe*); the tongue tip touches or comes near the upper teeth. Do not replace them with /t/ or /d/ unless you are consciously marking a contrast: **tin/thin**, **day/they**, **breed/breathe**. /z/ is a voiced sound made with the tongue behind the teeth: **breathe/breeze**, **close** (verb) /kləʊz/ and **clothe** /kləʊð/. (3) /w/ rounds the lips (*wet, wear, well*); /j/ raises the tongue toward the hard palate (*yet, year, yell*). Minimal pairs: **wet/yet, wear/year, well/yell**.\n\n**Practice:** listen to a dictionary recording or your teacher, then say each pair twice: leisure/ledger; tin/thin; day/they; breathe/breeze; wet/yet; wear/year. Write the first sound of *measure, jam, think, that, yell, wine* as an IPA symbol.\n\n**Answers:** /ʒ/, /dʒ/, /θ/, /ð/, /j/, /w/. For production, the teacher checks that voiced sounds vibrate lightly at the throat and that /w/ and /j/ are different.\n\n**Assessment:** written identification 6 marks. Pronunciation is assessed separately by listening; accent variation is respected, and the target is the reference contrast, not the learner's background.",
