+    "title": "Consonant contrasts: /t/ and /s/; /s/ and /z/; the nasals /m/, /n/ and /ŋ/",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** hear and say /t/ against /s/, /s/ against /z/, and the three nasal consonants; explain how the spelling *ng* relates to /ŋ/.\n\n**Teaching:** (1) **/t/ and /s/.** /t/ is a stop: the tongue blocks the air and releases it. /s/ is continuous friction. Minimal pairs: **tip/sip, tea/sea, tie/sigh**. (2) **/s/ and /z/.** /s/ is voiceless; /z/ is voiced. The spelling letter *s* can stand for either: **sip/zip, peace/peas, price/prize, rice/rise, face/phase**. A final /z/ can shorten the preceding vowel. (3) **Nasals.** /m/ closes the lips, /n/ closes the tongue tip against the ridge, and /ŋ/ closes the back of the tongue against the soft palate. Minimal triples: **sum/sun/sung, ram/ran/rang, sin/sing**. /ŋ/ does not begin English words. In *sing* and *singer* the *ng* is a single sound /ŋ/ with no separate /g/; in *finger* and *longer* the /ŋ/ is followed by /g/.\n\n**Practice:** say each pair twice with a recording: tip/sip; peace/peas; rice/rise; sum/sun/sung; sin/sing. Mark the sound at the end of *ran, rang, ram*. Decide whether *singer* and *finger* rhyme on their first syllable.\n\n**Answers:** /n/, /ŋ/, /m/. *Singer* /ˈsɪŋə/ and *finger* /ˈfɪŋɡə/ do not rhyme exactly because *finger* has /g/.\n\n**Assessment:** written identification 5 marks; production observed separately by a listening teacher. The scheme's printed symbols are not recoverable in the original document, so these are the contrasts named by their words (“/t/ and /s/”, “/s/ and /z/”, “/m/, /n/ and /ŋ/”).",
