+    "title": "Vowel contrasts: /ʌ/ and /ɑː/; /æ/ and /ɜː/; /ɔː/ and /ɒ/; /ʊ/ and /uː/; schwa /ə/",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** hear and say five vowel contrasts in the British reference model; locate schwa in unstressed syllables; link a vowel's length and quality to spelling without treating spelling as proof of sound.\n\n**Note on the scheme:** the Second-Term document prints some of these symbols in ASCII or with substituted characters (“/^/ and /a:/”, “/æ/ and /ӡ:/”). They are read here as /ʌ/ and /ɑː/, /æ/ and /ɜː/. This is an inference from the typed forms.\n\n**Teaching:**\n- **/ʌ/ and /ɑː/:** short *cup, cut, luck, hut*; long *cart, lark, heart, car*. Minimal pairs: **cut/cart, luck/lark, hut/heart**.\n- **/æ/ and /ɜː/:** *cat, bad, ban, hat* against *curt, bird, burn, hurt*.\n- **/ɔː/ and /ɒ/:** long *caught, short, port, stalk* against short *cot, shot, pot, stock*.\n- **/ʊ/ and /uː/:** short *full, pull, look, could* against long *fool, pool, Luke, cooed*.\n- **Schwa /ə/:** the weak vowel of unstressed syllables, as in *about, teacher* /ˈtiːtʃə/, *banana, support, today*. Many small words reduce to schwa when unstressed: *a* /ə/, *to* /tə/, *of* /əv/. Stress controls it: **photograph** /ˈfəʊtəɡrɑːf/ but **photography** /fəˈtɒɡrəfi/.\n\nDo not treat length marks as decoration: the **ː** shows a longer, usually tenser vowel.\n\n**Practice:** say each pair twice with a recording: cut/cart; cat/curt; shot/short; full/fool. Mark every schwa in “The teacher asked about a banana.” Say the words *about, of, today* in isolation and in a sentence and describe the difference.\n\n**Answers:** schwa in *the* (before a consonant), *teacher* (second syllable), *about* (first), *a*, *banana* (first and last). In isolation *of* is /ɒv/ but in a sentence usually /əv/. Marking guide: accept a correct set if the learner names the stressed and unstressed syllables.\n\n**Assessment:** written 6 marks; production and listening checked by a teacher or recording. Accent variation is normal; assess the contrast against the stated reference model.",
