+    "title": "Formal letter: layout, tone and a complete model",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** set out a formal letter correctly; use a polite, impersonal tone; contrast formal and informal letters; revise a draft against a checklist.\n\n**Teaching:** A formal letter is written to an official, an employer, a principal or an organisation. Its parts, in order: **sender's address** (top right), **date**, **receiver's title and address** (left), **salutation** (“Dear Sir/Madam” or “Dear Mr Bello”), **subject line** (“Application for the post of …”), **body** in short paragraphs (reason, details, request or action), **closing** and **signature**. Use “Yours faithfully” after “Dear Sir/Madam” and “Yours sincerely” after a named person. Informal letters use a friendly opening, contractions and personal news; formal letters use complete sentences, no slang and no contractions.\n\n**Model (original):**\n14 Unity Road,\nIbadan.\n18 October 2026.\n\nThe Principal,\nHillview Secondary School,\nIbadan.\n\nDear Sir,\n\n**Request for permission to use the school library on Saturdays**\n\nI am a JSS 3 student writing to request permission to use the school library on Saturday mornings. Many of us have no quiet place to study at home before the examination period.\n\nIf permission is granted, I will be responsible for keeping to the opening hours and for returning every book on the same day. I would be grateful for your consideration.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAyo Adeyemi\n(JSS 3 B)\n\n**Practice:** (1) Name the part that tells the reader what the letter is about. (2) Which closing follows “Dear Madam”? (3) Rewrite the informal sentence “Hi! Just wanted to ask if we can have a trip” as a formal sentence. (4) Write a formal letter of about 120 words asking the vice-principal for a debating club meeting room. Use the full layout.\n\n**Answers:** (1) the subject line (2) Yours faithfully (3) “I am writing to ask whether the class may be permitted to organise a trip.” (4) Marking guide (15 marks): layout 5, tone and formality 4, clear request and reason 3, spelling and punctuation 3. Penalise slang and contractions in a formal text.\n\n**Assessment:** a layout checklist and a short drafted letter. Real-world acceptance by a school office is not tested.",
