+    "title": "Essay writing review: narrative, descriptive and argumentative structure",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** plan and structure a narrative, a descriptive and an argumentative essay; write an introduction, body paragraphs and conclusion; revise using a short marking guide.\n\n**Teaching:**\n- **Narrative essay:** tells what happened in time order. Parts: setting and characters (opening), problem or event (build-up), turning point (climax) and result or lesson (ending). Use past tense, a clear order and a few vivid details.\n- **Descriptive essay:** describes a person, place or thing so the reader can picture it. Plan by sense or by position: sight, sound, smell, touch, taste; or near to far. Use specific nouns, adjectives and comparisons. Do not tell a story.\n- **Argumentative essay:** argues one side. Parts: **introduction** states the topic and your position; **body** has one reason per paragraph with a supporting example; one paragraph answers an opposing view; **conclusion** restates the position. Use linking words such as *firstly, moreover, however, therefore, in conclusion*.\n\n**Model plan (original) — “Should students use phones in school?” (argumentative):** Introduction: phones help and distract, but strict rules are needed. Reason 1: access to dictionaries and learning apps. Reason 2: safety and contact with parents. Opposing view: distraction in class, answered by locked-away phones during lessons. Conclusion: allow phones only under school rules.\n\n**Practice:** (1) Which type of essay uses mainly the senses? (2) Which type needs a conclusion that restates a position? (3) Write a plan for a descriptive essay “My favourite subject” with three paragraphs. (4) Write a 150-word narrative beginning: “The generator coughed and the lights went out.” (5) Mark your own draft against the guide.\n\n**Answers:** (1) descriptive (2) argumentative (3) Marking guide: introduction naming the subject, body paragraphs on sights/sounds/feelings or on topics and teachers, conclusion giving the reason it is favourite. (4) Marking guide (10 marks): clear time order 3, interesting details 3, tense control 2, spelling and punctuation 2. (5) Check the draft for introduction, body, conclusion and linking words.\n\n**Assessment:** the pupil's own draft marked with the guide. The model plans are original teaching examples, not past examination scripts.",
