+    "title": "Critical reading: purpose, facts and opinions, and word meaning in context",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** state a writer's purpose; separate fact from opinion; work out a word's meaning from its context; read critically by asking what evidence supports a claim.\n\n**Teaching:** Before reading, ask: *Why was this written — to inform, to persuade, to entertain or to describe?* While reading, mark **facts** (can be checked: “The school has 600 students”) and **opinions** (judgements: “The school is the best in the town”). Opinion signals: *I think, in my view, best, worst, should, probably*. To find a word's meaning in context, read the clause before and after it, look for synonyms, opposites or examples, and test your guess by putting it in place of the word.\n\n**Passage (original):** “The new market stalls opened on Monday. There are forty of them, and every stall has a roof. I believe this is the best change the council has made in years, because traders no longer sell in the rain. Some people say the rent is too high; that complaint is exaggerated. A shopper walking through the lane saw that the aisles were wide enough for two carts and that vendors were unusually cheerful.”\n\n**Practice:** (1) What is the writer's main purpose? (2) Give one fact and one opinion. (3) What does *exaggerated* probably mean? (4) What does *unusually* tell you about the vendors' mood? (5) What question would you ask to test the writer's claim that this is “the best change”?\n\n**Answers:** (1) to persuade readers that the new stalls are a good change (2) Fact: there are forty stalls (or every stall has a roof). Opinion: “the best change the council has made in years”. (3) made to seem bigger or more serious than it is, from the contrast with “complaint” and the writer's dismissal (4) the vendors were cheerful more than normal (5) Accept: what evidence compares this change with earlier changes, and how do traders and shoppers feel? Marking: one mark each, with an extra mark for giving a reason in (3).\n\n**Assessment:** 6 marks. The passage is original teaching material, not an examination passage.",
