+    "body": "**Objectives:** practise the question types that appear in school English examinations — comprehension, vocabulary, grammar, speech work and literature — and check answers against a marking guide.\n\n**Status:** these are new practice questions written for revision. They are **not** past examination questions and do not reproduce any examination board's paper. Third-Term BECE scheduling belongs to a later batch; this resource is a tagged practice set for that later use and is placed here only so it can be reached now.\n\n**Comprehension (original passage):** “Ade's bicycle had a flat tyre on the morning of the examination. He did not panic. He pushed it to a repairer and waited, though he knew he might arrive late. When he reached school, the examiner had not yet distributed the papers. He sat down breathless but calm.”\n1. Why did Ade not panic? 2. What does *distributed* mean? 3. Pick out the adjective in the last sentence.\n\n**Grammar and vocabulary:** 4. Change to the passive: “The repairer mended the tyre.” 5. Add a question tag: “Ade is punctual, ___?” 6. Choose the synonym of *calm*: (tense / peaceful / loud). 7. Give the opposite of *panic*.\n\n**Speech work:** 8. Which word has the vowel /ɜː/: *cat, bird, hot*?\n\n**Literature:** 9. Define *theme*. 10. Name the rhyme scheme of this couplet: “The rain is soft, the evening long, / We sit together, sing a song.”\n\n**Answers:** 1. He knew the problem could be solved; accept a sensible reason from the text. 2. given out, handed out 3. breathless (or calm) 4. The tyre was mended by the repairer. 5. isn't he 6. peaceful 7. stay calm, relax (accept *calm*) 8. bird 9. the central idea or message 10. AA (a rhyming couplet). Marking: one mark each, 10 in total.\n\n**Assessment:** 10 marks. A set of original questions practises the skills but does not prove knowledge of an examination paper. Authentic past papers still need lawful source provenance.",
