+    "title": "Àṣà ìgbéyàwó: marriage in Yoruba culture and the steps the school lists",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** explain what ìgbéyàwó means in Yoruba culture; name the school's listed steps; explain, with evidence, the four steps that credible sources describe (ìwádìí, alárinà, ìtọrọ, ìdána); note that the other steps are named but not explained here.\n\n**Teaching:** Ìgbéyàwó is the union of a man and a woman as husband and wife in line with Yoruba custom. The school note gives six values that support a good marriage: ìfẹ́sọ́nà (courtship), ìfaradà (perseverance), sùúrù (patience), ìpamọ́ra (tolerance), ìgbọ̀ràn-ẹni-yé (understanding) and ìfẹ́ àìsetàn (unconditional love).\n\nThe school's list of fifteen steps is printed in plain spelling: *ifojusode, iwaadi, alarina, isihun, ijohen, itoro, baba gbo iya gbo, idana, ipalemo, ifa iyawo, ojo igbeyawo, ese iyawo wiwe, ibale, ikorun, ojo keje*. The school's tone marks and the exact spelling of some entries are not recoverable, and different Yoruba towns and families order and practise the steps differently.\n\n**Steps explained from credible sources:**\n- **Ìwádìí** — inquiry: the groom's family finds out about the woman's family, character and background.\n- **Alárinà** — the go-between (the word means “one who goes between”): a trusted person who carries messages between the two families.\n- **Ìtọrọ** — the formal introduction and request: the groom's family comes to ask for the woman in marriage; it is described as the first part of the traditional marriage.\n- **Ìdána** — the ceremony in which the groom's side pays the bride-price and presents gifts; the West African Examinations Council examiners' report names it as a distinct step in the traditional marriage process.\n\n**Not explained here:** *ifojusode, isihun, ijohen, baba gbo iya gbo, ipalemo, ifa iyawo, ese iyawo wiwe, ibale, ikorun* and *ojo keje*. The supplied school note names them but gives no explanation, and no credible source was found that matches each of these names. A Yoruba teacher or elder must explain them before they are taught. Do not invent a meaning.\n\n**Practice (ìbéèrè):** (1) Define ìgbéyàwó. (2) Name three of the six values of a good marriage. (3) Explain the role of the alárinà. (4) What happens at ìdána? (5) Why should a pupil say “the families I know do it this way” rather than “all Yoruba do it this way”?\n\n**Answers:** (1) the union of man and woman as husband and wife by Yoruba custom (2) any three of the values above (3) a go-between who carries messages and helps the two families talk (4) bride-price and gifts are presented and the families confirm the arrangement (5) because customs differ by town and family. Marking: one mark each.\n\n**Assessment:** 5 marks. Written recall does not replace learning from elders or participation in actual ceremonies.",
