+    "title": "Ìrànra-ẹni-lọ́wọ́: practice on cooperative work and thrift",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** revise the Yoruba ways of helping one another (ọwẹ́, àáró, àjọ, ẹ̀ṣúsú, owó ẹlé, san-án-díẹ̀-díẹ̀, fífi ọmọ tàbí ǹkan ìní dúró); explain each with one example; use them correctly in sentences; judge how modern life changes them.\n\n**Teaching (revision of the school's list):**\n- **Ọwẹ́** — a communal work gathering: a person invites relatives, friends and neighbours to help with a big task, usually farm work. It is repaid by joining others' ọwẹ́ and by hosting.\n- **Àáró** — a rotating work arrangement among friends: each person's farm is worked on by the group on one day until every member has had a turn. Food or drink is not required, but the work must go round.\n- **Àjọ** and **ẹ̀ṣúsú** — contribution savings: members each contribute a fixed amount at set intervals and each in turn receives the whole collection. Trust and a trusted collector are essential.\n- **Owó ẹlé**, **san-án-díẹ̀-díẹ̀** and **fífi ọmọ tàbí ǹkan ìní dúró** — borrowing and repayment practices named in the school's list. The last historically meant giving a child or property as security for a debt; teach it as history and discuss why a child must not be treated as security today.\n\n**Open item:** the scheme's word *obese* (First-Term week 5) is not in the school's own explanation and its intended meaning is not established. It is not guessed here. Ask a Yoruba teacher what the school intended; if the intended item is a thrift or work practice, add it to this list.\n\n**Practice (ìbéèrè):** (1) Match: ọwẹ́, àáró, ẹ̀ṣúsú with (a) friends take turns working on each other's farms, (b) neighbours are invited to help with a large task, (c) members each give money and take turns to receive the total. (2) Give one benefit of group work. (3) Give one risk of ẹ̀ṣúsú. (4) Explain why a person's child cannot be used as security today. (5) Name a modern form of cooperative saving.\n\n**Answers:** (1) ọwẹ́–(b), àáró–(a), ẹ̀ṣúsú–(c). (2) work is done faster and bonds are strengthened. (3) a member may fail to pay or the collector may misuse the money. (4) children have rights and are not property; modern law and values forbid it. (5) a cooperative society, a credit union or a savings group. Marking: one mark each, with a reason expected in (4).\n\n**Assessment:** 5 marks. Tone marks on the Yoruba terms need a Yoruba teacher's check, because the school source is printed without them.",
