+    "title": "Ọbalúayé (Ṣọ̀pọ̀ná): a bounded alternative reading of the scheme's 'obalale'",
+    "body": "**Objectives:** profile Ọbalúayé as one possible reading of the scheme's unclear *obalale*; compare him with Ọbàtálá; show how a source word that cannot be confirmed is handled honestly.\n\n**Status:** the existing lesson on Ọbàtálá reads the scheme's *obalale* as Ọbàtálá. The word is also close to **Ọbalúayé**, so this lesson gives the second reading. It does not decide which one the school intended. A Yoruba teacher must confirm.\n\n**Teaching:** Ọbalúayé (also Ọbaluaiyé, Ṣọ̀pọ̀ná, in the Americas Babalú-Ayé or Omolu) is an òrìṣà associated with the earth, disease and healing. Sources describe him as feared for the diseases he is believed to bring, especially smallpox, and respected for the power to heal and remove epidemics. His shrines are traditionally placed at the edge of a town. The name Ṣọ̀pọ̀ná is considered a name not to be spoken lightly, so other names and titles are used. Compare with Ọbàtálá, who is described in the existing lesson as the creator of human bodies, with white as his colour. Both are òrìṣà in Yoruba tradition; the two are different figures with different domains.\n\n**Practice (ìbéèrè):** (1) Name two other names for Ọbalúayé. (2) State two areas of life associated with him. (3) Why are the shrines of Ọbalúayé usually at the edge of a town according to the source? (4) Explain how Ọbalúayé and Ọbàtálá differ. (5) Why can this lesson not close the scheme item?\n\n**Answers:** (1) Ṣọ̀pọ̀ná and Babalú-Ayé (or Omolu) (2) disease and healing, the earth (3) the source says the shrines are typically on the outskirts and are linked to preventing epidemics (4) different domains: Ọbalúayé with disease, earth and healing; Ọbàtálá with the making of human bodies and the colour white (5) the intended òrìṣà of the school word is unconfirmed. Marking: one mark each.\n\n**Assessment:** 5 marks. The lesson reports published descriptions of belief and does not ask a learner to hold or reject those beliefs; respect learners of different faiths.",
