+    "body": "**Objectives:** read any Yoruba literature book (prose, drama or poetry) with a repeatable method; summarise its story or content in correct order; describe characters with proof from the text; state the lesson (ẹ̀kọ́) of the book and support it.\n\n**Important status:** the First-Term scheme asks for a supplementary Yoruba literature book and the Second-Term scheme for the book chosen by the government. **Neither title is named in the supplied materials.** This lesson is a text-independent method. It does not teach the school's book, it does not claim to be the government's prescribed list, and the existing story *Ramúramù ewúrẹ́* and the reading lesson *Ìwé tí ó sọnù* remain labelled selections, not the school's set text.\n\n**Teaching (method):**\n1. **Ìtàn (story or content):** before reading, note the title and author. After each chapter or act, write one sentence saying what happened.\n2. **Ẹ̀dá ìtàn (characters):** choose two characters. Write one quality for each and one action or line of speech that proves it. A quality without proof is only an opinion.\n3. **Place and time:** say where and when the events happen and how they affect the story.\n4. **Ẹ̀kọ́ inú ìtàn (lesson):** state the lesson in one sentence and give one event that supports it. For poetry, say what each stanza adds and name one image.\n5. **Language:** copy one proverb, idiom or expression from the book and explain its meaning in your own words. If you are unsure of a tone mark, ask the teacher before copying it.\n\n**Practice (ìbéèrè):** use your set text, or paste a short passage into the Study my text screen. (1) Write five sentences telling the story in order. (2) Describe one character with proof. (3) State the lesson of the book and one supporting event. (4) Explain one proverb or idiom from the text.\n\n**Answers (marking guide, 10 marks):** story order 3, character with proof 3, lesson with supporting event 2, proverb or idiom explained 2. Reject a lesson that is only a one-word topic.\n\n**Assessment:** teacher marking of the learner's own notes. This method does not prove that the prescribed text was read, and typed-text study does not replace the missing set-text list.",
