+    "body": "**Objectives:** name and mark the three Yoruba tones; show how a change of tone changes meaning; count syllables in Yoruba words; recognise the three syllable shapes (V, CV and syllabic N).\n\n**Teaching:** Yoruba has three tones. **Ohùn òkè** (high) is marked with an acute accent (´), **ohùn ìsàlẹ̀** (low) with a grave accent (`), and **ohùn àárín** (mid) is normally left unmarked. The tone is part of the word. Examples: **igba** (two hundred, all mid), **ìgbà** (time), **igbá** (calabash). Another set: **ọkọ̀** (vehicle), **ọkọ́** (hoe), **ọ̀kọ̀** (spear) and **ọkọ** (husband). Writing the tone marks is therefore not decoration; leaving them out can change the word.\n\nA **sílébù** (syllable) is the smallest part of a word you can say in one breath; the number of syllable-carrying sounds shows the number of syllables. The shapes are **V** (a vowel alone: *a*, *ẹ*, *ọ*), **CV** (a consonant and a vowel: *wá*, *lọ*, *kọ̀*) and **N** (a syllabic nasal, *n* or *m*, carrying a tone: *ń* in *Mo ń lọ*). Count: **ọmọ** = ọ-mọ (2); **ilé** = i-lé (2); **Ìbàdàn** = Ì-bà-dàn (3); **Mo ń lọ** = mo-ń-lọ (3).\n\n**Practice (ìbéèrè):** (1) Name the three tones and the mark for each. (2) Give the meaning of *igba*, *ìgbà* and *igbá*. (3) Mark each syllable of *ìgbà* as high, mid or low. (4) Count the syllables in *àgbàdo* and *Ọ̀yọ́*. (5) Why is the *ń* in *Mo ń lọ* a syllable?\n\n**Answers:** (1) high (´), low (`), mid (unmarked) (2) two hundred, time, calabash (3) ì – low, gbà – low (4) àgbàdo = à-gbà-do (3); Ọ̀yọ́ = Ọ̀-yọ́ (2) (5) a syllabic nasal can carry its own tone. Marking: one mark each, 5 total.\n\n**Assessment:** written marking, 5 marks. Tone marks and any spoken tone are best checked by a competent Yoruba speaker; written accuracy does not prove spoken accuracy. Tone marks in this lesson must be checked by a Yoruba teacher before release.",
